(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-general-dermatology-i-quiz"]={"title":"General Dermatology I Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which scale classifies skin type by its response to ultraviolet light?","choices":["The Fitzpatrick scale","The SCORTEN score","The Auspitz sign","The Nikolsky sign"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — six types, from always burns and never tans through to never burns. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which finding should raise concern for deep venous thrombosis in a patient with suspected stasis dermatitis?","choices":["Acute unilateral swelling or pain","Bilateral chronic hyperpigmentation","Pruritus with scaling","Atrophie blanche"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Other considerations include peripheral arterial disease, vasculitis, lymphoedema and cutaneous malignancy. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"What underlies xerosis, and who is most affected?","choices":["An autoimmune process affecting the epithelial basement membrane, occurring most often in the elderly and remitting spontaneously after five or six years","Impaired stratum-corneum hydration promoted by ageing, low humidity, hot water, detergents, atopy and systemic disease, commonly affecting older adults especially in winter","Overgrowth of Malassezia in sebum-rich areas, in young men","Chronic venous hypertension in the lower legs, in older adults"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, causing tightness, pruritus, rough scale, fissuring or eczema. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 152"},{"q":"What distinguishes a plaque from a patch?","choices":["A plaque is always poorly demarcated while a patch is always well demarcated","A plaque is elevated and plateau-like; a patch is flat with no elevation","A plaque is larger than a patch","A plaque is fluid-filled and a patch is solid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — both exceed one centimetre, so elevation is the discriminator. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Is urushiol contact dermatitis contagious?","choices":["No, but it may spread to other areas or people if urushiol is not removed from skin or clothing","Yes, direct contact with the rash transmits it","Yes, but only during the vesicular stage","No, and the resin cannot be transferred once it has dried onto clothing or bedding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the rash itself does not spread person to person; the resin does. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"How is pemphigus initially treated?","choices":["With ultrapotent topical steroids alone","With watchful observation alone, since the disease usually remits spontaneously within five to six years without treatment","With oral doxycycline as monotherapy","Urgently, with rituximab or high-dose oral prednisone, often alongside azathioprine or mycophenolate so steroids can be tapered off"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is a life-threatening disorder, so treatment is urgent. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"What is direct immunofluorescence used to diagnose in this lecture?","choices":["Dermatophyte infection, by showing fungal elements within the stratum corneum","Herpesvirus infection, by showing multinucleated giant cells in a smear","Scabies infestation, by showing the mite, its eggs or its faecal pellets","Autoimmune blistering disease, by showing where antibody is deposited in the skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is what separates bullous pemphigoid from pemphigus on a biopsy. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which first-line topical agent for mild plaque psoriasis acts quickest, and what is its caution?","choices":["Salicylic acid — caution with systemic absorption in children","Coal tar — caution with photosensitivity","Methotrexate — caution with hepatotoxicity","Calcipotriene, a vitamin D analogue — caution with nephrotoxicity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside emollients, topical steroids and ultraviolet B phototherapy as first line. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 163"},{"q":"Which formulation is generally most occlusive, and what is it the foundation of treatment for?","choices":["Gels, because they dry quickly and do not trap heat","Ointments, and they are the foundation of treatment for many inflammatory conditions including xerosis, eczema, irritant dermatitis and diaper dermatitis","Lotions, used chiefly for scalp conditions","Creams, because they spread most easily over large areas and are the foundation of treatment for xerosis, eczema, irritant dermatitis and diaper dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — ointments are more occlusive than creams or lotions. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which treatment is contraindicated in pregnancy for pustular psoriasis, and what is used instead?","choices":["Phototherapy is contraindicated in pregnancy; acitretin is used instead","Biologics are first line in pregnancy","Acitretin is contraindicated; high-potency topical steroids are used if pregnant","Methotrexate is safe in pregnancy and is preferred"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 164"},{"q":"How is mild perioral dermatitis treated once the corticosteroid is stopped?","choices":["Topical ketoconazole","Oral prednisone","Topical metronidazole, erythromycin, pimecrolimus or azelaic acid","A higher-potency topical corticosteroid applied twice daily"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with oral tetracycline or doxycycline for more extensive or persistent disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"What does this lecture say about a patient presenting with purpura?","choices":["It is a benign finding common in the elderly","It requires only outpatient dermatology follow-up","It is diagnostic of thrombocytopenia","It is a medical emergency until proven otherwise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture flags it explicitly rather than treating it as one finding among many. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How is a macule defined in this lecture?","choices":["An elevated solid lesion less than one centimetre","A flat elevated plateau lesion measuring greater than one centimetre across","A flat lesion less than one centimetre, without elevation or depression","A flat lesion less than five millimetres"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A patch is the same lesion larger than one centimetre. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of alopecia areata, and why is the hair loss non-permanent?","choices":["Repeated traction pulls the follicle from the scalp","Autoreactive T cells infiltrate the follicle and switch it from growing to resting, without destroying the stem cell compartment","Autoantibodies destroy the hair follicle and its entire stem cell compartment, so that any regrowth is never possible","Dihydrotestosterone progressively miniaturises the follicle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the stem cells survive, so regrowth remains possible. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 137"},{"q":"How does the timing of irritant contact dermatitis vary with the irritant?","choices":["All irritants produce symptoms 48 to 72 hours after exposure regardless of whether the agent is a mild soap or a concentrated acid","Timing does not vary with the irritant","Mild irritants such as soaps may progress over weeks, while alkalis and acids may present within minutes or be delayed up to 24 hours or more","All irritants produce symptoms within minutes of exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the agent determines the tempo. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What are the four Ps of lichen planus?","choices":["Purple, painless, peripheral pustular lesions","Pruritic, purple, polygonal, papules or plaques","Painful, pale, punched-out papules","Pruritic, pustular, patchy plaques"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — flat-topped, pink to violaceous and shiny, coalescing into larger plaques. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"What is the main differential for nummular eczema, and what distinguishes them?","choices":["Tinea corporis — tinea has central clearing and nummular eczema does not","Psoriasis — psoriasis has silvery scale and nummular eczema does not","Pityriasis rosea — pityriasis has a herald patch","Contact dermatitis — contact dermatitis is well demarcated"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and this single feature is the discriminator the lecture gives. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"How is atopic dermatitis diagnosed?","choices":["By skin biopsy in every case","By serum immunoglobulin E level, which is measured routinely in every patient and establishes the diagnosis on its own without any clinical assessment","By patch testing in every case","Clinically, supported by family history, personal atopy, recurrent rash and elevated immunoglobulin E, though routine laboratory testing is not required"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patch testing is reserved for atypical, adult-onset or treatment-resistant disease, and biopsy for atypical or refractory disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Where is minoxidil primarily effective?","choices":["Only in women","In the crown region of the scalp","At the frontal hairline","Over the entire scalp equally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it prevents further loss and may produce regrowth, working best with finasteride. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"What does light microscopy show in bullous pemphigoid?","choices":["Band-like infiltration of neutrophils throughout the papillary dermis","Budding yeast and pseudohyphae","Neutrophils aligned in a straight narrow row at the dermal-epidermal junction","Acantholysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy of a lesion goes for histopathology and perilesional tissue for direct immunofluorescence. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of pemphigus?","choices":["Autoimmune damage to the epithelial basement membrane producing subepithelial blistering that resolves without any scarring","A type IV delayed hypersensitivity reaction to an external allergen","Impaired stratum-corneum hydration","Loss of keratinocyte-to-keratinocyte adhesion, called acantholysis, from autoantibodies to adhesion molecules, producing intraepithelial blisters"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a life-threatening blistering disorder of skin and mucous membranes. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 129"},{"q":"What is the cornerstone of management for stasis dermatitis, and what must be established first?","choices":["Compression therapy, once adequate arterial circulation has been established","Compression therapy, applied immediately in all patients on presentation","Long-term systemic antibiotics","High-potency topical corticosteroids as monotherapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — an ankle-brachial index or toe pressure is considered when arterial status must be clarified before substantial compression. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"How does eczema change in appearance from acute to chronic?","choices":["Acute shows erythema, oedema, papules, vesicles, oozing and crusting; chronic shows xerosis, fissuring, lichenification and pigment alteration","Acute disease shows lichenification and pigment alteration; chronic disease shows erythema, oedema, vesicles, oozing and crusting","The appearance is fixed and does not evolve over time","Acute shows pigment loss; chronic shows vesiculation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with a subacute stage of scaling, erythema, papules and excoriations between them. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which statement about topical antifungals is correct?","choices":["They are effective for scalp and nail infections without any need for systemic therapy, and are the mainstay of treatment for allergic contact dermatitis","They are ineffective against dermatophytes","They are first-line for allergic contact dermatitis","They are particularly effective against dermatophytes, but topical therapy is inadequate for most scalp or nail infections"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — terbinafine, clotrimazole and ketoconazole are the agents named. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which four features separate pemphigus from bullous pemphigoid?","choices":["Subepithelial rather than intraepithelial, flaccid rather than tense blisters, a positive Nikolsky sign, and no acantholysis on biopsy","Intraepithelial rather than subepithelial, flaccid rather than tense blisters, positive rather than negative Nikolsky sign, and acantholysis on biopsy","Subepithelial, tense blisters, negative Nikolsky sign and acantholysis","Intraepithelial, tense blisters, negative Nikolsky sign and no acantholysis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the comparison table is the highest-yield slide in this section. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 131"},{"q":"How is a poorly demarcated lesion described?","choices":["Irregular or blotchy in appearance, without well-defined borders","Smaller than one centimetre in every dimension measured","Present in more than one body region","Raised above the surrounding skin surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — as against a well demarcated lesion, which has clearly defined borders. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What does biopsy show in lichen planus?","choices":["Band-like infiltration of lymphocytes in the dermis","Acantholysis","Neutrophils in a row at the dermal-epidermal junction","Budding yeast and pseudohyphae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with hyperkeratosis without parakeratosis, basal layer vacuolisation, Civatte bodies and a saw-tooth rete ridge. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 177"},{"q":"How do seborrhoeic dermatitis lesions appear, and where?","choices":["Coin-shaped plaques without central clearing on the extremities","Tense bullae on the trunk and flexures","Poorly demarcated pruritic erythematous plaques 5 to 20 mm, greasy with flaking or yellow scaling, in areas rich in sebaceous glands","Well-demarcated salmon plaques with prominent silvery scale on the extensor elbows, knees, trunk, scalp, umbilicus and sacrum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — scalp, eyebrows, under facial hair, nasolabial creases, forehead, nose, behind the ears and the external auditory canal. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Why does the medication history ask when each drug was started in relation to the rash?","choices":["Because only prescription medications can cause eruptions","Because the dose determines whether a reaction is possible","Because the timing between starting a drug and the eruption is what implicates it","Because most drug eruptions occur only after six months of continuous therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a new rash after starting a medication, especially with fever or mucosal sores, is an urgent warning sign. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Where does dyshidrotic eczema occur, and what are its vesicles classically called?","choices":["On the scalp, with vesicles described as exclamation point lesions","On the palms, lateral and dorsal fingers and soles, with intensely pruritic vesicles described as tapioca vesicles","On the flexural surfaces of the elbows and knees, with vesicles described as herald patches preceding the wider eruption","On the extensor surfaces, with vesicles described as tense bullae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vesicles may coalesce into bullae, then desiccate and resolve with desquamation. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 65"}]};
